<title>MEXICO: Mexico private sector sees fewer '97 grain imports.</title>
<headline>Mexico private sector sees fewer '97 grain imports.</headline>
<dateline>GUADALAJARA, Mexico 1997-04-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>Mexican imports of basic grains will fall by 31 percent in 1997 compared with levels from the previous year, the private sector National Agriculture Council  CNA said in a report on Wednesday.</p>
<p>The report said imports in 1997 will be about 7.53 million tonnes of beans, wheat, barley, corn, rice and sorghum, compared with a total 10.76 million tonnes exported in 1996.</p>
<p>Corn saw the largest decline in import levels, followed by wheat, according to the report. The CNA said corn imports should fall because of a larger expected national harvest.</p>
<p>The following table shows a breakdown of grain import levels in tonnes estimated for 1997 and from 1996.</p>
<p>					1997		    1996</p>
<p>    Corn			 2,800,000	     5,965,000</p>
<p>    Sorghum		    2,000,000	     1,983,000</p>
<p>    Beans			  124,000		 127,000</p>
<p>    Wheat			2,000,000	     2,060,000</p>
<p>    Barley			 290,000		 300,000</p>
<p>    Rice			   320,000		 329,000</p>
<p>    Total			7,534,000	    10,764,000</p>
<p>((--Monica Ballesca, Guadalajara newsroom +523 817-0397))</p>
